Here's how to implement this in .NET MAUI Shell:

## 1. Custom "Cancel" Back Button

In your `EditProductPage.xaml`:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="YourApp.EditProductPage"
             Shell.BackButtonBehavior="{StaticResource CancelBackButton}">

    <ContentPage.Resources>
        <BackButtonBehavior x:Key="CancelBackButton"
                            TextOverride="Cancel"
                            Command="{Binding BackCommand}" />
    </ContentPage.Resources>

    <!-- your form here -->
</ContentPage>
```

## 2. Intercept Navigation with Confirmation Dialog

In your code-behind or ViewModel, handle both the custom button and the hardware/gesture back:

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    public bool HasUnsavedChanges { get; set; }

    public ICommand BackCommand { get; }

    public EditProductPage()
    {
        BackCommand = new Command(async () => await TryGoBack());
        BindingContext = this;
        InitializeComponent();
    }

    // Intercept hardware/swipe back (Android back button, iOS swipe)
    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (HasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            // Fire-and-forget the dialog; return true to cancel navigation
            MainThread.BeginInvokeOnMainThread(async () => await TryGoBack());
            return true; // suppress default back
        }

        return false; // allow normal navigation
    }

    private async Task TryGoBack()
    {
        if (HasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                "Unsaved Changes",
                "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
                "Discard", "Stay");

            if (!leave)
                return; // user chose to stay
        }

        await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`BackButtonBehavior`** — replaces the shell back arrow with a "Cancel" label and routes taps through your `BackCommand`.
- **`OnBackButtonPressed()`** — intercepts the Android hardware back button and iOS swipe gesture. Return `true` to cancel navigation.
- Both paths funnel into `TryGoBack()`, which shows the confirmation dialog and only navigates if the user confirms.

> **Note:** `OnBackButtonPressed` doesn't fire for the iOS swipe-back gesture in all Shell scenarios. If that's critical, you may also need to disable the swipe via `Shell.BackButtonBehavior` with `IsVisible="True"` (which disables the default gesture) or by setting `IsEnabled` conditionally.
